{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/session-dabb45d0-review.md", "content": "# Session review: task-dabb45d0

The stream included edge bars and isolated/motif colors, but the sole supplied demo that changed cells changed only two adjacent 5s to 6s (demo 1: rows 2–3, column 1 in zero-based coordinates); demos 2–4 changed only small portions: demo 2 changed three 7s in a 7-bar to 4s, demo 3 changed a 3x2 patch of 8s to 7s, and demo 4 recolored a compact 5-shape to 6. These are distinct local recolorings, not evidence for projecting broad edge rays. Repeated submissions that altered a guessed 3-color edge pattern failed, illustrating the need to fit a selector or relation to all demos before transfer.

Operational lesson: compute each demo's exact delta, including changed old/new colors and unchanged candidates; do not infer common mechanics just from edge bars or marker presence. If hypotheses remain unvalidated, a demo is useful only if its geometry discriminates them. Do not submit a rule unsupported by exact demo replay. This note does not establish a solved transformation for this task.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/session-dabb45d0-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-demo-discipline", "file": "references/task-dabb45d0-review.md", "content": "# Review: task-dabb45d0

This stream illustrated a process failure: despite having three demo pairs, actions repeatedly consisted of reference rereads and another demo request rather than an explicit delta ledger. Demo 1 changed only two cells: color 3 at (2,1) and (3,1) became 6; prominent bars and other shapes stayed fixed. Demo 2 changed three cells: the three 7s in row 4, columns 11–13 became 4; all other content stayed fixed. Demo 3 changed four cells: the 8s at (2,17),(2,18),(3,17),(4,18) became background 5; rest unchanged. These edits differ in object/color/geometry, so they do not by themselves establish one generalized transformation. In particular, the test's conspicuous 3, 7, and 8 formations cannot safely be modified by projecting, recoloring, or stamping based on one example.

Reusable lesson: after each pair, enumerate exact changed coordinates and colors immediately; test a measurable selection rule against all demos and unchanged candidates. A prior note about a matching task ID is cautionary context, not a task rule. If current examples do not validate a shared rule, spend a demo only to test a stated distinction; do not take repeated reference reads as progress. A candidate output must replay every known demo exactly before transfer.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-stream-demo-discipline/references/task-dabb45d0-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/compact-output-reassessment-after-errors.md", "content": "# Compact-output reassessment after incorrect submissions

When inputs are partitioned by repeated separator rows or columns but demos produce much smaller outputs, dimension ratios help identify possible structure but do not establish the encoding. Keep distinct hypotheses for (a) summarizing interior regions, (b) recording separator/boundary markers at their intersections, and (c) selecting only rows or columns with special markers. For each demo, write down the exact partition counts and map candidate input coordinates or blocks to each output cell, including ordering. Reproduce every output exactly before transferring the map.

After an incorrect compact-output submission, do not merely alter colors or copy another visually salient set of markers. Reopen the task class and output representation, and re-derive dimensions and correspondence from all demos. Repeated, heterogeneous demos may share only an abstract structure; spend another demo only when two explicit mappings predict a materially different discriminating observation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/compact-output-reassessment-after-errors.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/demo-output-mapping.md", "content": "# Validate the demo's output mapping before transfer

A visually structured demo output is not evidence by itself for reflection, tiling, or stamping. After the first plausible but incorrect submission, reopen the task class and output representation before changing coordinates. Record input/output dimensions, map input rows, columns, objects, or symbols to output cells/regions, and compare that candidate mapping against every available pair. Treat exact full-demo reproduction as a prerequisite for submitting; a coherent pattern that fails even one demo is still an unvalidated guess. If requesting another demo, identify which competing mappings its predicted output would distinguish.
