{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/session-f0caf098-compact-output-diagnostic.md", "content": "# Compact-output diagnostic: distinguish tile summaries from noise

In a fresh Continual-ARC instance, the test was a 22×27 partitioned canvas with compact separator-defined regions, but six requested demos had unrelated palettes and dimensions, each yielding compact outputs (examples included 3×4, 3×4, 2×6, 6×2, 5×4, and 1×1). The demos may share the abstract representation of compressing a partitioned canvas, but no exact mapping from each demo's tile contents to output values was derived. Two speculative test submissions were wrong; subsequent requests did not resolve the transformation. This is a diagnostic, not a solved rule.

Reusable decision: prioritize output dimensions and potential partition-to-output mapping over salient test markings. For each demo, count tile rows/columns and map each candidate tile feature (marker color, marker presence, or pattern) to the corresponding output cell; test exact predictions across all pairs. If demonstrations vary, distinguish stable abstract layout from changing tile-level encoding instead of declaring them unrelated at a glance. Never infer test output from output dimensions alone. After a wrong submission, reopen representation and task class, and don't consume further demos without a named ambiguity and different predicted evidence. In any case, the user's interaction contract requires exactly one valid action JSON as the last line on every puzzle response; ensure even a process-limit summary contains a legal action, and do not claim the instance is resolved.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/session-f0caf098-compact-output-diagnostic.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/session-dabb45d0-drift-lesson.md", "content": "# Session lesson: demo drift and unsupported transfers

This instance began with a test grid featuring sparse shapes and edge bars. The demos varied widely in dimensions, background colors, and visual content. Some showed only small localized recolorings (for example a 5→6 patch, a few 7→4 cells, or 8→7 cells), while another changed a compact 5-shape to 6; these deltas did not validate any shared ray, stamp, or edge-bar rule for the test. Repeatedly recoloring salient test motifs without an exact cross-demo rule led to incorrect submissions.

Reusable lesson: track each pair's exact changed-cell mask and old→new colors before hypothesizing. Changing dimensions/backgrounds and unrelated local deltas are evidence to reconsider task identity/distribution, not a license to generalize from shared colors or bars. After a miss, drop the unsupported rule rather than make a cosmetic variation. Request another demo only if a specific predicted delta would discriminate surviving hypotheses; otherwise preserve the remaining demo budget.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/session-dabb45d0-drift-lesson.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/abstract-rule-vs-demo-heterogeneity.md", "content": "# Distinguish visual heterogeneity from task-rule drift

A fresh stream used demos with very different dimensions, palettes, backgrounds, and motifs from the test. The demos' observed edits were themselves disparate localized edits: demo 1 changed a pair of 5s to 6s; demo 2 changed a single 8 to 9; demo 3 changed a 7-cell to 9; demo 4 changed a local 7 region to 8; demo 5 changed two 7s to 3; demo 6 recolored a compact 2x3 patch from 2 to 5. No shared selector or abstract transformation was derived, and repeated requests were made without analyzing a cumulative delta ledger. The session's final candidate merely recolored the test's 8 structures to 4 without support from the demos.

Reusable inference lesson: neither assume that different-looking demos are unrelated nor force a common rule based on colors/salience. Precisely record each pair's delta, object context, and unchanged candidates; then seek measurable invariant relations (shape, relative position, local neighborhood, object role) across examples. Test any proposed rule by exact full-output replay on every demo. If deltas remain unlinked, further demos require a specific competing-hypothesis prediction; serial requests do not resolve uncertainty by themselves. For the test, keep its actual colors and structures unchanged unless a validated rule predicts edits.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/abstract-rule-vs-demo-heterogeneity.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-demo-discipline", "file": "references/grid-serialization-validation.md", "content": "# Grid serialization check

A visually copied or manually assembled output can be invalid even when the task rule is understood. Before submission, verify mechanically that the output has exactly the expected number of rows and that each row has exactly the expected number of cells. Check values are integers in the allowed range, then serialize the same validated grid. Do not infer successful dimensionality from the visual layout: inspect all row lengths, since one omitted background cell in a row is enough to make a nominal 19x17 output malformed.
